+This method initializes the data structures required for tiled parallel compositing by computing the spatial extent (boundaries) of each tile within the framebuffer [G]. It solves the problem of determining which input primitives or data elements contribute to which output tiles, enabling subsequent parallel processing stages to operate on disjoint subsets of the image without synchronization [G]. It differs from sibling methods like [Coarse Raster](2807-coarse-raster.md) by focusing on the setup of the boundary metadata itself rather than the rasterization of geometry, and from [Dynamic Load Balancing via Tile Queue](1763-dynamic-load-balancing-via-tile-queue.md) by being a static setup phase rather than a runtime scheduling mechanism [G]. Choose this during the initialization phase before parallel execution begins.
 
 ### [Parallel Image Assembly](3271-parallel-image-assembly.md)
-Parallel Image Assembly is the final step in spatial decomposition volume rendering where sub-images rendered by independent threads or processes are combined into a single final image. It solves the problem of merging disjoint spatial data portions into a coherent visual output by placing sub-images at their correct spatial coordinates and blending them along the z-axis using front-to-back alpha compositing. Unlike temporal sequencing, which has minimal overhead, spatial compositing incurs significant overhead due to the data collection and blending requirements.
+Parallel Image Assembly is the final step in spatial decomposition volume rendering where sub-images rendered by independent threads or processes are combined into a single final image. It solves the problem of merging disjoint spatial data portions into a coherent visual output by placing sub-images at their correct spatial coordinates and blending them along the z-axis using front-to-back alpha compositing. Unlike temporal sequencing, which has minimal overhead, spatial compositing incurs significant overhead due to the data collection and blending requirements. Choose this when combining results from spatially partitioned rendering passes.
 
 ### [Multi-Threaded Tile-Based Rendering](3784-multi-threaded-tile-based-rendering.md)
-Multi-Threaded Tile-Based Rendering is a parallelization strategy that divides the screen height into tiles and processes each tile in a separate thread to parallelize the rasterization workload. It differs from [Tile-Based Alpha Compositing](1215-tile-based-alpha-compositing.md) by focusing on the rasterization phase itself rather than just compositing pre-rasterized fragments, and from [Screen Parallel Rendering](5725-screen-parallel-rendering.md) by dividing the screen into tiles processed by threads on a single machine rather than subscreens distributed across multiple processors [G].
+Multi-Threaded Tile-Based Rendering is a parallelization strategy that divides the screen height into tiles and processes each tile in a separate thread to parallelize the rasterization workload. It differs from [Tile-Based Alpha Compositing](1215-tile-based-alpha-compositing.md) by focusing on the rasterization phase itself rather than just compositing pre-rasterized fragments, and from [Screen Parallel Rendering](5725-screen-parallel-rendering.md) by dividing the screen into tiles processed by threads on a single machine rather than subscreens distributed across multiple processors [G]. Choose this when offloading rasterization to CPU threads.
 
 ### [Tile-Based Wave Synchronization](5069-tile-based-wave-synchronization.md)
-Manages tile assignment and synchronization across worker threads using wave indices and tokens [G]. It sets itself apart from lock-free tiling by providing explicit synchronization points for tasks requiring global consistency or progress tracking [G]. Choose this when implementing a path tracer or ray caster that requires wave-based synchronization [G].
+Manages tile assignment and synchronization across worker threads using wave indices and tokens [G]. It sets itself apart from lock-free tiling by providing explicit synchronization points for tasks requiring global consistency or progress tracking [G]. Choose this when implementing a path tracer or ray caster that requires wave-based synchronization.
 
 ### [Parallel Binary-Swap Compositing](10844-parallel-binary-swap-compositing.md)
-Parallel Binary-Swap Compositing is a method for combining independently rendered image tiles from multiple processors in a massively parallel environment. It solves the synchronization and communication bottlenecks of naive parallel compositing by reducing the data volume at each step, ensuring all processors remain busy with useful work. Unlike approaches where communication dominates or processors idle, this method maintains full processor utilization by pairing processors in a binary-swap fashion.
+Parallel Binary-Swap Compositing is a method for combining independently rendered image tiles from multiple processors in a massively parallel environment. It solves the synchronization and communication bottlenecks of naive parallel compositing by reducing the data volume at each step, ensuring all processors remain busy with useful work. Unlike approaches where communication dominates or processors idle, this method maintains full processor utilization by pairing processors in a binary-swap fashion. Choose this in distributed or multi-GPU environments where inter-processor communication is a bottleneck.
+
+### Tile-Grouping Architecture
+A tile-grouping architecture that merges adjacent tiles into larger work units, reducing redundant sorting and improving data reuse within GPU streaming multiprocessors [S2, unofficial]. It sets itself apart from single-tile processing by increasing the working set size per multiprocessor to hide memory latency and improve cache hit rates [S2, unofficial]. Choose this when targeting modern GPU architectures with large shared memory and high memory bandwidth.
 
